---
title: Accessibility
description: How Formsnap associates labels, descriptions and error regions with controls, and how to choose an announcement policy.
---

<script>
	import { Callout } from "$lib/markdown";
</script>

Formsnap's job is association: the `for`/`id` pair between a label and its control, `aria-describedby` for descriptions and errors, and the live-region policy for error announcements. Getting those right is the difference between a form that reads correctly in a screen reader and one that looks correct and is silent.

## Owned content slots

Use the owned content slots on `Field`, `ElementField`, or a native `Fieldset` when associations must exist in server-rendered HTML, including with JavaScript disabled. The scope knows which containers it will render before it renders any control, so child order does not affect the associations.

| Prop              | Behavior                                                                                                                     |
| ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `description`     | A content snippet. Omitted means no description container and no reserved ID.                                                 |
| `fieldErrors`     | `true` renders the default error content; a snippet renders custom content. Omitted or `false` renders no error container.    |
| `descriptionId`   | Optional ID override for an enabled owned description. An ID alone does not render or associate a container.                  |
| `fieldErrorsId`   | Optional ID override for an enabled owned error region. An ID alone does not render or associate a container.                 |
| `fieldErrorsLive` | Announcement policy for an enabled owned error region: `"assertive"` (default), `"polite"` or `"off"`.                        |

```svelte title="src/routes/settings/settings-form.svelte"
<Field {form} name="email" fieldErrors>
	<Control>
		{#snippet children({ props })}
			<Label>Email</Label>
			<input type="email" {...props} bind:value={$formData.email} />
		{/snippet}
	</Control>
	{#snippet description()}
		We'll only use this to confirm your account.
	{/snippet}
</Field>
```

`Control` hands you `props` with `id`, `name`, `aria-describedby`, `aria-invalid` and `aria-required` already resolved. Spread them onto whatever element renders the control.

## Generated IDs and lifetime

Generated IDs belong to the component instance, not the field path, so repeated fields and multiple forms on one page have separate targets. ID generation is stable between server rendering and hydration — nothing backpatches the DOM after first paint.

Enabled error containers stay mounted while empty, but their IDs enter `aria-describedby` only while that field has errors. A conditional snippet can be withdrawn by passing `undefined`; its association is withdrawn with it. Changing an enabled region's ID updates both the container and its controls.

## Custom error content

Custom error snippets receive `{ errors, errorProps }`. Render only the content — Formsnap owns the outer container, its ID and its `data-fs-field-errors` attributes. Spread `errorProps` onto each error item to keep `data-fs-field-error`, which styling and tests rely on.

```svelte
{#snippet fieldErrors({ errors, errorProps })}
	{#each errors as error, index (index)}
		<p {...errorProps}>{error}</p>
	{/each}
{/snippet}
```

## Standalone containers

`Description` and `FieldErrors` also work standalone for freely composed layouts. Their associations are registered when the actual elements mount and withdrawn on unmount — they do not discover targets before server rendering.

To associate caller-owned containers without JavaScript, provide explicit container IDs and native `aria-describedby` on the input yourself, including an error ID only while that container is rendered and has errors.

<Callout type="note">

An `ElementField` uses its local description when present, otherwise its parent's description — including an owned parent description on the server. A native `Fieldset` places its owned containers inside the fieldset; its full-container `child` replacement cannot be combined with an enabled owned description or error region.

</Callout>

## Error announcements

An enabled error region is a live region: it announces errors that appear or change after the page has loaded — what client-side validation and enhanced submissions produce.

| Container                                                       | Prop              |
| --------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------- |
| owned region on `Field`, `ElementField` or a native `Fieldset`  | `fieldErrorsLive` |
| standalone `FieldErrors`                                        | `live`            |

Both take `"assertive"`, `"polite"` or `"off"`. Precedence, highest first: the prop, then an `aria-live` attribute passed with the other props, then `"assertive"`.

- `"assertive"` interrupts whatever the screen reader is reading. It is the right default for an error the user must act on before continuing.
- `"polite"` waits for a pause in speech, which reads better for a message that changes on every keystroke.
- `"off"` renders `aria-live="off"` and suppresses announcements, including one implied by a `role` the caller put on a custom container. The error keeps its association, so a screen reader still reaches it when the user moves to the control.

<Callout type="warning">

Never let two live regions announce the same message. Field errors plus a form-wide error summary announce twice. Either keep the field errors `"assertive"` and give the summary no live region of its own, or set one to `"off"` and let the other own the announcement. See [Form-wide error summaries](/docs/recipes/error-summary).

</Callout>

## Keyboard responsibilities for custom controls

When you take over the markup with the headless hooks, you also take over the interaction contract:

- Every interactive element must be reachable with <kbd>Tab</kbd> in visual order.
- A custom control that is not a native input needs a `role`, an accessible name, and <kbd>Enter</kbd>/<kbd>Space</kbd> activation.
- Focus must remain on the control while its value changes; do not move focus to an error region on every keystroke.
- Keep error text out of the announcement path when a summary already announces it.

